      <h2>Central Heating in Liverpool's Victorian and Period Properties</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Liverpool has lots of older homes. Central heating in these takes real know-how.</p>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-navy" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>What Makes Liverpool's Older Properties Different</h3>
        <p>Solid brick walls. No cavity. Tight floor voids. Chimney breasts used as flue routes. Room shapes that are not standard. Older Liverpool homes have all of these. Areas like Aigburth, Allerton and Woolton are full of them.</p>
        <p>An installer with no local know-how can mis-quote these jobs. Or route pipes in ways that damage the period features. We plan every pipe route to keep your home's features safe. We aim to keep visible pipework low.</p>
        <p>Solid walls can make standard pipe boxing stick out. We talk through other routing options at the survey. We never cut corners that would hit long-term system life. We never damage features that cannot be put back.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-orange" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>First-Time Central Heating in a Liverpool Property</h3>
        <p>Has your Liverpool home never had central heating? A full fit from £3,500 is one of the best home jobs you can do. Homes with no central heating are a hard sell or hard let. A new system adds warmth. It boosts the EPC rating. It meets buyer hopes. It often adds more value than it costs.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://www.energysavingtrust.org.uk/advice/boilers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Energy Saving Trust</a> lists central heating as one of the best upgrades you can do. It boosts home energy use. It cuts yearly heating bills. We do free surveys for homeowners thinking of a fit before sale or rent.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
